The nested if statements here could be simplified. Since `version !== "latest"` and `version === "latest"` are mutually exclusive, this could be restructured as early returns:

```ts
if (!modExists || !cachedVersion) {
  // continue to install
} else if (version !== "latest" && cachedVersion === version) {
  return mod
} else if (version === "latest") {
  const isOutdated = await PackageRegistry.isOutdated(pkg, cachedVersion, Global.Path.cache)
  if (!isOutdated) return mod
  log.info("Cached version is outdated, proceeding with install", { pkg, cachedVersion })
}
```

Or consider using early returns at the top level to reduce nesting. Just a suggestion - feel free to keep as-is if you find this clearer.

`exportToSkillDir` uses `skill.name` directly in `path.join(targetDir, ".opencode", "skill", skill.name)`. If a skill is created/loaded with a name containing path separators (e.g. `../`), this can write outside `targetDir` (directory traversal). Sanitize/normalize the directory name (e.g., slugify like `saveSkill` does, and/or reject names with `/` or `..`).
```suggestion
    // Sanitize skill.name to prevent directory traversal and invalid path characters
    const safeName = skill.name.replace(/[^a-z0-9-]/gi, "-").toLowerCase()

    const skillDir = path.join(targetDir, ".opencode", "skill", safeName)
```

