`afterEach` restores env vars via `Object.assign(process.env, originalEnv)`, but this does not remove env keys introduced during the test run (e.g. `HTTP_PROXY`), so proxy env vars can leak into other test files. Consider explicitly deleting the proxy-related keys in `afterEach` (or deleting any keys not present in `originalEnv`) before restoring.
```suggestion
  afterEach(() => {
    // Remove any env vars that were introduced during tests
    for (const key of Object.keys(process.env)) {
      if (!(key in originalEnv)) {
        delete process.env[key]
      }
    }
```

`afterEach()` restores env vars with `Object.assign`, but it doesn’t remove keys introduced by the tests. If the last test sets e.g. `HTTPS_PROXY`, it can leak into the rest of the test suite. Consider deleting any env keys not present in `originalEnv` (as done in `fetch-integration.test.ts`) before restoring.
```suggestion
    // Restore original env vars: remove keys added during tests, then restore originals
    for (const key of Object.keys(process.env)) {
      if (!(key in originalEnv)) {
        delete process.env[key]
      }
    }
```

