      sessions.push({ info: session, messageCount, lastMessage: last })
```

The change from `import.meta.resolve` (which threw errors) to `Bun.resolve().catch(() => {})` means that resolution failures are now silently ignored. The original code had a try-catch that ignored errors, but the new code returns `undefined` on failure. When `resolved` is `undefined`, the plugin entry is not updated (line 1220 checks `if (resolved)`), so unresolvable plugins will remain as-is in the array rather than being filtered out or causing an error. This behavior change should be documented or reconsidered - it may be better to filter out unresolvable plugins or at least log a warning.
```suggestion
          const resolved = await Bun.resolve(plugin, path.dirname(configFilepath)).catch((error) => {
            // Log a warning instead of silently ignoring plugin resolution failures
            if (log && typeof (log as any).warn === "function") {
              ;(log as any).warn("Failed to resolve plugin", {
                plugin,
                configFilepath,
                error,
              })
            }
            return undefined
          })
```

When `Session.get()` is called with an invalid or non-existent session ID, it may return `null` or throw an error. The code does not handle this case and will likely fail with unclear error messages. Consider adding explicit error handling to provide a user-friendly message when a session is not found, such as "Session not found: {session_id}".
```suggestion
    const session = await Session.get(params.session_id)
    if (!session) {
      throw new Error(`Session not found: ${params.session_id}`)
    }
```

